[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:The Butterfly Effect]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.40]Lesson 14 | 第14课
[00:01.84]The Butterfly Effect | 蝴蝶效应
[00:08.25]Why do small errors make it impossible to predict the weather system with a high degree of accuracy? | 为什么微小的误差使我们无法高度准确地预报天气系统？
[00:17.46]Beyond two or three days, the world's best weather forecasts are speculative, | 超过两三天，世界上最好的天气预报也只是推测，
[00:23.20]and beyond six or seven they are worthless. | 而超过六七天，它们就毫无价值了。
[00:26.84]The Butterfly Effect is the reason. | 蝴蝶效应就是原因。
[00:30.02]For small pieces of weather-- | 因为对于小块的天气——
[00:32.07]and to a global forecaster, small can mean thunderstorms and blizzards-- | 而对一个全球性的预报员来说，“小”可能意味着雷暴和暴风雪——
[00:37.13]any prediction deteriorates rapidly. | 任何预报都会迅速失灵。
[00:40.35]Errors and uncertainties multiply, cascading upward through a chain of turbulent features, | 误差和不确定性成倍增加，通过一连串湍流特征向上积聚，
[00:46.71]from dust devils and squalls up to continent-size eddies that only satellites can see. | 从尘暴和狂风直到只有卫星才能看见的洲际规模的大旋涡。
[00:53.91]The modern weather models work with a grid of points of the order of sixty miles apart, | 现代天气模型以间隔约60英里的网格点来运作，
[00:59.92]and even so, some starting data has to be guessed, | 即便如此，有些起始数据也只能靠猜测，
[01:04.08]since ground stations and satellites cannot see everywhere. | 因为地面站和卫星不可能看到每个地方。
[01:08.69]But suppose the earth could be covered with sensors spaced one foot apart, | 但假设地球可以布满间隔一英尺的传感器，
[01:13.41]rising at one-foot intervals all the way to the top of the atmosphere. | 从地面一直以一英尺的间隔向上排到大气层的顶端。
[01:19.00]Suppose every sensor gives perfectly accurate readings of temperature, | 假设每个传感器都给出温度、
[01:24.13]pressure, humidity, and any other quantity a meteorologist would want. | 气压、湿度和气象学家想要的任何其他量的完全精确的读数。
[01:30.61]Precisely at noon an infinitely powerful computer takes all the data and calculates what will happen at each point | 正午时分，一台威力无穷的计算机取用所有数据，计算出每个点上将要发生什么，
[01:39.25]at 12.01, then 12.02, then 12.03... | 在12:01，然后12:02，然后12:03……
[01:45.38]The computer will still be unable to predict whether Princeton, New Jersey, will have sun or rain on a day one month away. | 计算机仍然无法预测一个月以后的某一天，新泽西州的普林斯顿是晴天还是雨天。
[01:54.42]At noon the spaces between the sensors will hide fluctuations that the computer will not know about, | 正午时，传感器之间的空隙会隐藏计算机无法知晓的起伏，
[02:01.52]tiny deviations from the average. | 即对平均值的微小偏离。
[02:04.37]By 12.01, those fluctuations will already have created small errors one foot away. | 到了12:01，这些起伏就会在一英尺之外造成小的误差。
[02:11.94]Soon the errors will have multiplied to the ten-foot scale, and so on up to the size of the globe. | 不久误差就会成倍放大到十英尺的尺度，如此下去，直至达到全球的规模。
